		;======================================================================; Revision History for the ProDOS FST.;; Feb. 01 1988  By Rob Turner;;               Forced dirty bitmap blocks to be written to disk after;               change_path call has been made.;;               Files changed:;                       (1) change.path;; Feb. 03 1988 By Rob Turner  v00.02 a01;;               Changed the chk_protect routine.  The routine use to;               issue a status call to the drive on every call that;               involved writing.  The routine now checks the VCR;               to see if we have already determined that the media;               is write enabled. If the media is write enabled then;               we just exit.  The status bit that determines if we;               should status the drive is cleared by the swap_out;               routine in SCM.;;               Files changed:;                       (1) common.code;                           changed the chk_protect routine.;; Feb. 08 1988 By Rob Turner  v00.02 a02;;               Changed the setup_parms routine.  On any write call;               the mount_volume routine was being called if the media;               was write enabled.  The reason for this was the mask used;               to determine of the volume had been swapped was wrong.;               instead of ANDing with $4000 to see if the swapped bit;               was set, the routine was ANDing with $BFFF, the mask used;               to clear the swapped bit.;;               files changed:;                       (1) common.code;                           changed setup_parms rouitne.;; Feb. 11 1988 By Rob Turner v00.02 a03;;               Fixed bug in the Read routine.  The flush file routine;               is called at the beginning of the read routine to see;               if the file needs to written to disk before the read;               takes place.  This routine may set the drvr_call_num;               to write. Later in the routine the dev_with_mount routine;               is called with drvr_call_num still set to write.  So instead;               of reading data into the user's buffer, the data from the;               user's buffer is written to the file he/she is trying to;               read.  Note: I now only call flush_file if the file is dirty;               this keeps use from flushing dirty bitmaps when we do not;               need to!;;               files changed:;                       (1) Read;                           changed dev_with_mount to read_with_mount;; Feb. 11 1988 By Rob Turner v00.02 a04;;               Fixed a bug in class 1 calls.  Drvr_fst_num was not setup;               on class 1 calls.;;               files changed:;                       (1) Common.code;                           Move drvr_fst_num setup to common position.;; Feb. 12 1988 By Rob Turner v00.02 a05 ---> v00.03 a01;;               Changed the way that flush_bitmap works.  Flush_bitmap;               now checks to see if the open_cnt for the vcr is zero.;               If the count is non-zero the bitmap block is not written;               to disk.  I also changed the routines that must write;               the bitmap to disk requardless of the open_file count.;;               files changed:;                       (1) io.subroutines;                           changed flush_bitmap, added force_bitmap;                       (2) bitmaps;                           calc_free: converted to force_bitmap;                           get_bit_block: converted to force_bitmap;                       (3) memory;                           remove_fcr: now calls force_bitmap.;; Feb. 22 1988 By Rob Turner v00.03 a02;;               Fixed a bug in create.  When creating an extended file;               the system could crash.  The problem occured when the;               data fork was not the same storage type as the resource;               fork.  The reason the system crashed was we were trying;               to create a sapling file with one block.  This will cause;               an index that is used to decrement to $FFFF, which in turn;               will walk its way down to zero, zapping everything in it's;               path.;;               files changed:;                       (1) create;                           changed the create_resource routine to setup;                           blks_used1 before calling the make_fork routine.;; Feb. 22 1988 By Rob Turner v00.03 a03;;               Fixed a bug in change path.  No error was returned if the;               destination file already existed.;;               files changed:;                       (1) change.path;                           changed 'end_change' to save the error code;                           before calling flush bitmap.  The error code;                           is now restored before calling 'main_exit';; Feb. 24 1988 By Rob Turner v00.03 a04;;               Changed the create call.  The create call use to set;               the minimum version to zero (SOS compatible) for all;               files.  I now set the minimum version to 5 (ProDOS;               FST) for extended files.;;               files changed:;                       (1) create;                           add a line of code that sets both version and;                           minimum version to Pro_ID.;; Feb. 25 1988 By Rob Turner v00.03 a05  --> v00.04 a01;;               Changed the seperator used in pathnames from a $80 to;               a ':'.;;               files changed:;                       (1) equ.and.data;                           changed delimiter equate from $80 to $3A (:);; Mar. 02 1988 By Rob Turner v00.04 a02;;               Changed the read routine to transfer small read request;               locally, instead of calling the move_info routine.;               This might speed things up.;;               files changed:;                       (1) read;                           added local move code 'send_small';                       (2) equ.and.data;                           added 'min_send_cnt' to equates;; Mar. 04 1988 By Rob Turner v00.04 a03;;               Change_path will now change the '*' prefix if we are;               renaming the boot volume.;;               files changed:;                       (1) change.path;                           added 'chk_boot_vol' routine;; Mar. 15 1988 By Rob Turner v00.04 a04;;               Change Format/Erase.  The volume name syntax is now checked;               before the format call is issued.;;               files changed:;                       (1) format.erase;; Mar. 16 1988 By Rob Turner v00.04 a05;;               Fixed a bug in set_eof.  When the file was converted;               from a tree to a sapling or from a sapling to a seedling;               the last index block entry was not being set to zero.;               So if you tried to write to the file without closing it;               you would run into many problems.;;               files changed:;                       (1) set_eof;                           added code to clear last index block in both;                           'tree_sapling' and 'sapling_seed'.;; Mar. 18 1988 By Rob Turner v00.04 a06;;               Fixed a bug in change path.  If you were moving a file;               with a subset name into a lower level folder I would;               return an error.  Example:;;               Path1   :foo:prodos.fst:debugger(0);               Path2   :foo:prodos.fst:debug:debugger(0);                                            ^;                                            | error occured here.  If I;                                              encountered a different char.;                                              and the destination char. was;                                              a delimiter I would return an;                                              error.  I should not return;                                              an error unless the source;                                              path terminated with a null.;               Example 2:;;               Path1   :foo:prodos.fst:debug(0);               Path2   :foo:prodos.fst:debug:debugger(0);;                       This example should return an error.;;               files changed:;;                       (1) change.path;                           added another check in 'subset_loop';; Mar. 19 1988 by Rob Turner v00.04 a07 --> v00.05 a01;;               Added FST specific call 'set_time_stamp'. Changed;               slug ripple to do the following based on 'time_stamp_opt':;;               Time_stamp_opt          Action;               --------------          ------;                       0               only files are time stampled;                       1               files + Subdirectories;                       2               files, Subdirs, subdirs to root level;;               added code to call 'slug_ripple' from the following calls:;;               1) Create;               2) Destroy;               3) Change_Path;               4) Set_File_Info;               5) close;               6) flush;;               fixed a bug in set_file_info.  If you set either the create;               or mod date to zero the current time was not used.;;               fixed a bug in 'read/write_with_cache'.  The carry flag;               was being srewed up on return do to a stack probelm.;;               fixed a bug in find_file.  If any part of the pathname;               was not found, a 'file_not_found' error was returned.;               The 'file_not_found' error is now returned only if the;               last name in the pathname is not found. Otherwise, a;               'path_not_found' error is returned;;               files changed:;                       (1) equ.and.data;                           new equates for fst specific calls;                       (2) data;                           new entry for fst specific call and added;                           close_flag, slug_block, slug_offset, time_stamp_opt;                       (3) fst.specific;                           this is new file;                       (4) create;                       (5) destroy;                       (6) change_path;                       (7) set_file_info;                           slug_ripple and changed 'get_pblk_time' to;                           handle reading the GS clock of the time is;                           zeros.;                       (8) close;                           close now sets up 'close_flag' to force a call;                           to 'slug_ripple';                       (9) flush;                           same as close;                       (10)common.code;                           setup_params now sets up 'close_flag' to bypass;                           a call to slug_ripple from flush_file.;                           added a routine called 'entry_to_offset'.;                       (11)io.subroutines;                           implemented slug_ripple and added code to;                           flush_file that will call slug_ripple if;                           time_stamp is enabled and close_flag is set;                           to true.;; Apr. 12 1988 by Rob Turner v00.05 a02;;               Changed max_pcount on format and erase to four;               files changed:;                       (1) data;; Apr. 16 1988 by Rob Turner v00.05 a03 --> v00.06 a01;;               Fixed a bug in Read.  I was checking the disk access bit;               for read enabled.  I should have been checking the;               user's access from the open call.;               Fixed a bug in Open.  If the user supplied the access on;               open, I did not verify the requested access with the;               actual access on disk, I now verify the requested access.;;               files changed:;                       (1) read;                           changed the check access from 'disk' to "user's";                       (2) open;                           changed the 'setup_access' routine.  The;                           routine now verifies the requested access;                           against the directory entry access.;; Apr. 21 1988 by Rob Turner v00.06 a02;;               Fixed a bug in set_eof.  When converting from Tree to sapling;               the buffers were loaded incorrectly.;;               files changed:;                       (1) set.eof;                           made changes to tree_sapling routine;                       (2) read;                           changed the fill_io_buf routine.  There was;                           a small bug that would refill an already sparse;                           block;;               fixed a bug in Format/Erase.  If the user erased a disk;               that had a VCR build then created a file on the freshly;               erased disk, the bitmap that was in memory would be;               written to the disk (unless the disk was switched).  To;               fix the problem I now call swap_out before formatting;               or erasing the disk.;; Apr. 22 1988 by Rob Turner v00.06 a03;;               Fix a bad bug in set_eof (I know, this routine is a real;               pain in the rear).  When converting from tree_sapling;               or sapling_seed the I/O buffer was being logically;               deallocated, and the I/O buffer was not loaded correctly;               on exit!;;               files changed:;                       (1) set.eof;                           changed 'tree_sapling' and 'sapling_seed';                           I now mark the index and master buffers;                           as clean instead of inactive.;; Apr. 25 1988 by Rob Turner v00.06 a04;;               Changed partion map name from 'prodos' to 'Apple_PRODOS';; May. 02 1988 by Rob Turner v00.06 a05 --> v00.07 a01;;               Fixed a bug in write.  The Index block loaded was;               always the index block number stored in the FCR.;;               files changed:;                       (1) write;; May. 05 1988 by Rob Turner v00.07 a02;;               Fixed a bug in create.  If the user specified only;               one of the date/time fields an incorrect date and;               time field were saved with the file.;;               files changed:;                       (1) create;                           now check both date and time fields.;;               Fixed bug in C1 Set_EOF.  The call was always returning;               param. out of range if any value greater then four;               was used in the displacement field.  The problem was;               caused by a bad equate.  The equate (index) included;               the PCount field in the class1 call.  The pointer that;               we use already points past the PCount field.;; May. 06 1988 by Rob Turner v00.07 a03;;               Changed the set_file_info routine.  The routine now;               forces the file type field to $0F if the storage type;               of the file is a directory.  This means that an;               application cannot set a directory to a ascii file etc.;; May. 19 1988 by Rob Turner v00.07 a04 --> v00.08 a01;;               Fixed a bug in the exit routine.  The routine was;               checking for certain errors so it could mark the volume;               "damaged".  However the error code had garbage in the;               high byte so the compare did not catch the error.;; Jun.	02 1988 by Rob Turner v00.08 a02;;		Changed the boot code to handle a sparse ProDOS file.;		This means if you initialize a disk then copy system;		disk 3.x it will now boot!  I changed this because;		the ProDOS FST automatically creates sparse files.;; Jun.	07 1988 by Rob Turner v00.08 a03 > v00.09 a01;;		Fixed a bug in the create call.  The create call was;		not allowing the invisible bit to be used as an input;		for "access".  Also fixed a bug in the set_fil_info call.;		The create and mod dates were not acting as described.;		Fixed a bug in the write routine.  If an error occured;		the transfer count, eof etc were not being saved.;; Jun. 	14 1988 by Rob Turner v00.09 a02;;		Fixed a bug in set_file_info (class 1)  The Pcount;		field was being decremented to often, causing the ;		create and mod dates to be missed.  Also, the order;		of the create/mod times were reversed, so the mod;		time was set even if the create time was the last;		parameter supplied.;;		Fixed a bug in change_path.  The rename bit was not;		honored by the call.  So any file could be renamed.;; Jun.	16 1988 by Rob Turner v00.09 a03;;		Fixed a bug in the Set_EOF call.  If you were reducing;		the EOF and the index block was sparse I would deallocate;		block zero.  Then I would try to deallocate block zero;		again and a damaged bitmap error would occur.;; Jun. 	23 1988 by Rob Turner v00.09 a04;;		Fixed a bug in set mark.  The call would allow you to;		set the mark to the same value as the EOF.;; Jun.	27 1988 by Rob Turner v00.09 a05;	;		Fixed a bug in the write routine.  The routine was not;		checking the size of the write to determine if the;		call would overrun the three byte EOF.;; Jun.	29 1988 by Rob Turner v00.09 a06;;		Fixed a bug in the write routine.  The I/O buffer was;		not being filled correctly if the file was a seedling file;		and you wrote $200 bytes to the file.;;		The "bug" fix in version 00.09 a04 was removed.  It turns;		out that it is a bug in P8 and P16.;;		Fixed a bug in block read/write.  These routines would;		put up a dialog indicating that a volume was damaged if;		the correct type of error was returned.  However, the;		volume name displayed did not match the volume that was;		in the disk drive.  This has now been fixed.;;		Fixed a bug in set_mark.  If you set the mark to the;		eof of a directory, the mark was being set to eof-1.;;; Jun. 30 1988 by Rob Turner v00.09 a07 > v00.10 a01;;		Fixed a bug in the write routine.  The last block of;		the file was trashed.  There has been major changes;		to make this work.;; Jul. 6 1988 by Rob Turner v00.10 a02;;		Fixed a bug in the create call.  If you were creating;		a resource file the blocks used field for the;		resource fork was taken from the data fork.;; Jul. 11 1988 by Rob Turner v00.10 a03;;		Changed an error code.  If you did a set_file_info;		call with an invalid access field, the error "invalid;		access" was returned.  The error should have been ;		"Parameter out of range";; Jul. 11 1988 by Rob Turner v00.10 a04 -> v00.11 b01;;		Making MAJOR changes to Read/Set_Mark.  I am trying;		to keep from writting dirty I/O buffers to disk;		when the current call will work from the dirty buffer;;		Added strip bit in FST header for pathnames.;; Jul. 18 1988 by Rob Turner v00.11 b02;;		Changed default for "time_stamp_opt".  The default;		now will time stamp files to the root level.;; Jul. 19 1988 by Rob Turner v00.11 b03;;		Fixed a bug in Get_Dir_Entry.  The high word of ;		the block count field was not being set to zero.;; Jul. 20 1988 by Rob Turner v00.11 b04 -> v00.12 b01;;		Fixed a bug in create.  An error code was being lost;		by one routine on exit;; Jul. 21 1988 by Rob Turner v00.12 b02;;		Fixed a bug in "Get_Dev_Num"  The routine was allowing;		a pathname to be used.;; Jul. 22 1988 by Rob Turner v00.12 b03;;		Fixed the FST header.  The max blocks was off by one.;; Jul. 24 1988 by Rob Turner v00.12 b04;;		Added the call "Get_Time_Stamp_Opt".  The new call;		is an FST specific call.  The call number is $8001;		All my fst specific calls will work as follows:;		Call numbers with high bit clear indicate input from;		caller.  Call numbers with high bit set indicate ;		status reguest from the call.;;		Changed the source file to get the common equaates from;		"common:common.equ";; Jul. 26 1988 by Rob Turner v00.12 b05;;		I now zero drvr_vol_id in setup_params.  This field will;		be setup by each call.;; Jul. 26 1988 by Rob Turner v00.12 b06;;		Change get_dir_entry.  Get_dir_entry was returning the;		blks_used and eof out of the directory block for all;		files.  This included extended files, which meant the;		eof was always $0200 and the blocks used was $0001.;		I now return the blocks used and eof of the data fork;		in the first eof and blocks used, and have extended;		get_dir_entry list to include resource eof and blocks;		used.  The new fields follow the option list pointer.;		below is a list of the new fields;;		Pcount		(now upto $0011);;		...		(normal parameters);;		option_list 	(4 byte pointer);		res_eof		(4 bytes);		res_blks_used	(4 bytes);;; Jul. 27 1988 by Rob Turner v00.12 b07 -> v00.14 b01 (big leap past v00.13);;		Fixed a major bug in set_eof.  If the file was seedling;		and the buffer was dirty, and both the mark and eof ;		were zero then a call to fill_io_buf would decrement;		the mark to $FFFFFFFF and fill the I/O buffer with;		zeros.  This followed by a flush_file would cause ;		block zero to be written to the disk.;; Aug. 01 1988 by Rob Turner v00.14 b01 -> v00.15 b01;;		NO CHANGES!;; Aug. 02 1988 by Rob Turner v00.15 b02;;		Changed read_block.  The routine will now allow you;		to read from a disk with a dirty bitmap or open files.;; Aug. 02 1988 by Rob Turner v00.15 b03;;		Change create.  The create call will no longer change;		the storage type to 'SubDir' if the file type is $0F;		(subdirectory).  This fixes Graphics Studio;; Aug. 04 1988 by Rob Turner v00.15 b04;;		I now allow full pathnames in the get_dev_num call;; Aug. 04 1988 by Rob Turner v00.15 b05 -> v00.16 b01;;		I now convert error "dev_not_found" to "invalid_dev_num";		on block read/write.;;		If the caller creates a file with a class one call and;		a pcount of 4 and a file Type of $0F (Subdir) I will;		adjust the storage type to be a subdirectory.  I will;		also set the file type to $0F (Subdir) if the storage;		type is set to "SubDir".  This means you cannot create;		a subdirectory with a filetype other than $0F.;; Aug. 04 1988 by Rob Turner v00.16 b02 -> v00.17 b01;;		Fixed a bug in set_file_info.  The call will now update;		both the disk and the FCR.  The call was not updating;		the FCR if the file was open.;; Aug. 11 1988 by Rob Turner v00.17 b02;;		Fixed a bug in Fill_io_buf. Once again Block zero was;		being written and a data block not being saved.  Thanks;		to Ed Magnin of Cinemaware for sending "C" source that;		would create the problem.;; Aug. 12 1988 by Rob Turner v00.17 b03 -> v00.18 b01;;		Flush_File and Flush_Block will no longer write sparse;		blocks to disk.  The routine should never be called;		with sparse blocks marked as dirty but this will cover;		my rear.;; Aug. 14 1988 by Rob Turner v00.18 b02 -> v02.00 (First Public Release Sys Disk 4.0);;		Changed "unpack_time".  The routine will now zero the;		date if the input date is invalid.;		Changed "get_pblk_time". The routine now validates the;		month.;;		Fixed a bug with the backup bit.  The backup bit was;		not being set correctly.  I now set the backup bit in;		The "flush_file" routine.;;		Fixed a bug in Create Extended.  If the total blocks;		overflowed to zero the system crashed.  The reason;		was that "Find_Free_blks" was being called with no;		blocks free on the disk.  This is a no-no.  Also fixed;		a bug in create extened, where the resource header block;		was not counted for when comparing the number of blocks;		free on the disk verse the number of blocks needed.;; Oct. 12 1988 by Rob Turner v02.01 a01;;		Fixed a bug in "unpack_time".  The day compare was off;		by one.  It should have been 32, not 31.  This was;		causing the 31st of any month to default to zero.;; Nov. 02 1988 by Rob Turner v02.01 a02;;		Fixed a bug in Remove.fcr.  The routine was;		decrementing the open file count in the VCR then calling;		"force_bitmap" to force any dirty blocks to be written;		to the disk.  ;;		The Problem:;;		Remove_FCR;		  --> Decrement the VCR open count. (1 => 0);		  --> Call Force_Bitmap;		   	--> Call Device Write (diskSwitch Error Here);			--> OS Calls Swap_out.;				--> Swap_out finds my VCR. (open count of 0);				--> Swap_out removes my VCR <-- (BUG BUG);			--> Mount Dialog is put up;			--> User Types OK;			--> Find_volume is called.;			--> The volume is found and a new VCR is built.;			--> Note: a blank Bitmap is loaded in VCR;		  	--> We now write data that was purged.;		  --> Remove the FCR;;		I now decrement the Open file count AFTER calling force_bitmap;;		I also changed Create.  The create call will now call;		force_bitmap instead of flush_bitmap.  Flush_bitmap;		does not write the bitmap if there are any open files;		on the volume, force_bitmap always write the bitmap;		to disk.;; Nov. 09 1988 by Rob Turner v02.01 a03;;		Changed the destroy routine.  The routine was setup to;		look for multiply resource forks, it now only deletes;		the two forks.  I also removed the debug code that;		zeroed every block after it had been deleted. Slow....;;		Changed the Create Call.  The create call will now allow;		you to add a resource fork to an existing file.  The;		only types of files that this is valid for is seedling;		sapling or tree.  Furthermore, only the storage type;		and resource_eof fields are used by the create call;		when an extension request has been made.  To extend;		a file, the application has to set the storage type;		to $8005 (Resource + $8000(extend bit)).  This new;		feature brings along with it two new error codes.;		The error codes are $70 (resource_exist: the file;		already contains a resource fork) and $71 (res_add_err:;		The file cannot have a resource fork added, e.g. a;		subdirectory).  The code is a major hack, so you are now;		warned.  The main code that was added relates to the variables;		named "expand_xxxxxx"  there are a few.;; Nov. 16 1988 by Rob Turner v02.01 a04;;		Fixed a problem with the Read Call.  The read call would;		only issue multiblock read request if the blocks that;		were sequential were less the $8000.;;; Dec. 02 1988 by Rob Turner v02.01 a05;;		Fixed a bug in the delete routine.  If you were deleting;		a resource file the delete call could trash your disk.;		the problem was that I tried to delete the resource;		fork assuming the resource fork had the same storage;		type as the data fork.  The problem code looked like;		the following:;		;			ldy	#$0100		;get the resource type;			lda	[ptr]		;I forgot the ',y';; Dec. 05 1988 by Rob Turner v02.01 a06;;		Changed the Open Call.  The call will now cache the;		first data block of the file. currently only the;		index blocks are being cached.;;		Fixed a bug in the Boot code.  The boot code was suppose;		to zero a block to handle sparse files.  The zero loop;		had a bug in it.;; Dec. 05 1988 by Rob Turner v02.01 a07;;		Change the "flush_file" routine so it would make the;		total blocks used field in the directory entry be;		the real total blocks for an extended file.;; Dec. 09 1988 by Rob Turner v02.01 a08;;		Changed the Format/Erase calls.  These calls were not;		checking for an error on the "Get_Block_Size" status;		call.  I also added range checking on the block size.;; Jan. 11 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a09;;		Fixed a bug in Get_Dir_Entry. The routine was not handling;		a bad entry name.  What was happening is the storage;		type was non zero but the name length was zero and I;		ended up transfering 64K bytes into the user's name;		buffer.;; Jan. 14 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a0A;;		Changed Fill_IO_Buf so that index blocks are also ;		put in the cache.;; Jan. 18 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a0B;;		Changed the open routine.  Now if the file is open for;		reading and another open call is made with;		"open_as_permitted" as the requested access I will open;		the file a second time giving read access to the caller.;		The last version would return an error.;; Jan. 19 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a0C;;		The FST will now only display the "Volume Damaged";		message once.;; Jan. 20 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a0D;;		Major changes.  I have added the field "dirty_file_cnt";		to the VCR.  I did this so I can flush the bitmap when;		the last file that is dirty is closed, instead of;		waiting till the last file is closed.;;		new labels to look for:;;			dirty_file_cnt;			chk_dirty_flag;			dirty_flags;			dirty_cnt_changed;; Jan. 23 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a0E;;		Get_File_Info now puts the key block of a resource;		file in the cache.;; Jan. 24 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a0F;;		Added the system global clean/dirty flag to the ;		file control record.  If the file is clean then;		the high bit of the access word is set to 1. If the;		file is dirty the high bit is set to 0.;; Jan. 25 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a10;;		Changed setup_params to not display damaged message.;; Jan. 25 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a11;;		Changed change_path.  I was not updating the file;		count in the VCR if a change path occured.;; Jan. 25 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a12;;		Changed change_path.  Added better use of the cache;		and other Operating system calls.;; Jan. 25 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a13;;		Optimized flush routine.;; Feb. 13 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a14;;		Fixed a bug in SetEOF.  If SetEOF changed the structure;		of the file, it did not update the directory entry;		until the file was closed.  This meant if you openned;		the file did a set EOF followed by a delete call, the;		disk would be damaged.;; Feb. 14 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a15;;		Fixed a bug in find_file.  The find_file routine was;		switching all registers to 8 bit mode when it was;		only suppose to switch the accumulator.  This caused;		the FST to fail with pathnames longer then 255;		characters.;; Feb. 15 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a16;;		Alloc FCR now passes the volume name for all open;		calls.  SCM will append any partial path to the;		volume name.;; Feb. 16 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a17;;		Added the resource_bit to the access word in the;		FCR.  The Resource_Bit(Bit 14) if set to one;		indicates that the file is an extended file.;; Feb. 20 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a18;;		Added seperator to volume name when I pass in the name;		to the Alloc FCR routine.;; Feb. 27 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a19;;		Fixed a bug in SetFileInfo.  The routine was not range;		checking the seconds and day of week fields.  This was;		not a problem since the ProDOS FST does not use the;		fields.;; Mar. 02 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a1A;;		Added option list support on all calls that have the;		parameter.  The first data word of the option list;		contains the FST ID.;; Mar. 03 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a1B;;		Fixed a bug in "File_Open_Routine".  The routine was;		checking the files Key_Block to detect that the file;		was opened.  However, the routine was NOT checking;		which volume the file was on.  This meant if a file;		was open on volume "A" and you tried to delete a file;		on volume "B" that had the same Key_Block, a;		"File_Open_Error" would be returned.;;		Fixed a bug in open.  If an error occured while;		sending the user information I was not removing the;		file control record.;; Mar. 07 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a1C;;		Added post_event code if a device is written too.  This;		will allow the Finder to detect volume change.;; Mar. 13 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 a1D;;		Fixed a bug where I was storing into the FCR even though;		the FCR pointer was not valid.;; Mar. 15 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b01;;		ProDOS FST now saves the case of file names.;		Added two new fst specific commands.  The new commands;		are:;		(1) Set_Upper_Case;		(2) Get_Upper_Case;; Mar. 16 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b02;;		The ProDOS FST will not set the mix case bit in the;		access byte if the name is all upper case or the FST;		specific option to disable case is enabled.;; Mar. 16 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b03;;		The mix case bits are now stored in the version and min;		version fields in the directory entry.;; Mar. 16 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b04;;		Format/Erase was not handling volume names that;		contained lower case letters.  This caused the ProDOS;		FST not to recognize the volume.;; Mar. 16 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b05;;		I no longer ask for the disk if the caller is doing;		a close call and there are no dirty files and the;		bitmap is clean.;; Mar. 20 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b06;;		Get_File_Info now calculates the mod time for the;		volume.;; Mar. 28 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b07;;		Fixed a major bug in the write routine.  It was;		possible to have a block of data repeated into the;		next data block by mistake.  I now copy the user's;		data into the I/O buffer even on multi-block transfer.;; Mar. 31 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b08;;		Fixed a bug in GetFileInfo.  An Error was returned;		when the PCount was 2 or 3.;; Apr. 03 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b09;;		Fixed a bug in Format/Erase.  The case bits were not;		being reset before the format/erase calls were made.;		This caused a problem if the new name was all uppercase.;; Apr. 07 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b0A;;		Fixed a bug in the ProDOS boot code.  The boot code;		was reading block zero into the buffer when a sparse;		block was encountered.;; May  10 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b0B;;		Fixed a bug in rename volume.  If the destination name;		was an active VCR in the system, I would build a new;		VCR with the same name.;; May  23 1989 by Rob Turner v02.01 b0C;;		Fixed a bug in rename volume.  If the destination name;		was the same as the source I was returning "duplicate;		volume" error.  I now return no error.;; May  30 1989 by Rob Turner v03.00;;		Fixed a bug in rename volume.  If the boot prefix ;		contained lower case characters the FST would not;		match names. Also, changed version to 3.0;; Jun  01 1989 by Rob Turner v03.01;;		Added Mod date to the volume.  The four bytes preceeding;		case bits in the volume header now contain the last;		mod date for the volume.  Note: the following is how;		the Mod date is calculated for a ProDOS volume:;;		(1)	Mode_date := Create_date_From_Vol_Header;		(2)	IF mode date <> NILL THEN;			Mode_Date := Mode_Date_From_Vol_Header;		(3)	WHILE NOT End_of_Vol_Dir DO;				BEGIN;				Temp_Mod := Next_Entries_Mod_Date;;				If Temp_Mod > Mode_Date THEN;					Mod_Date := Temp_Mode;				END;;		The directory scanning is used in case the disk was;		modified by SOS,ProDOS 8, ProDOS 16 or GS/OS version 4.0,;		all of which, do not support the Modification Date in the;		volume header.;; Jun  06 1989 by Rob Turner v03.02;;		Fixed a bug in ChangePath.  The routine that was checking;		the volume name against the boot volume name, was ;		checking one fewer bytes then it should have been.;; Jun  14 1989 by Rob Turner v03.03;;		Fixed a bug in the create call.  If the caller was;		adding a resource fork to an existing file, the create;		call required that their be enough free blocks to;		create the entire file from scratch. (It should check;		the storage needed to ADD the resource fork only.);; Jul  05 1989 by Rob Turner v03.04;;		Fixed a bug in GetDirEntry.  If a buffer size of 4;		was used, the FST walked over 64K of memory.  I also;		fixed a bug, where the BufTooSmall error was not being;		returned if I transfer the user's count and it was less;		then the file length.;; Aug  03 1989 by Rob Turner v03.05;;		Fixed a bug in the write call.  If the application;		issued a write call that caused the "partial_write";		routine to write across a block boundary, then;		complete the write with sparse data, followed by;		setting the EOF past the current mark, the file would;		be trashed!;; Aug  27 1989 by Rob Turner v03.06;;		Fixed a bug in Set_EOF. Set_EOF was releasing blocks;		from the current EOF to the new EOF, if the new EOF;		was less then the current EOF.  This was only a problem;		when the file was larger then the EOF (Caused by;		a create call with an EOF greater then zero).;		To fix this problem I added a routine called ;		"setup_real_eof" to the Set_EOF code.  This routine;		will determine the Current EOF based on physical;		allocation of blocks instead of logical EOF.;;		Also fixed a bug in "create".  The create call would;		create the wrong type file if you passed in an EOF;		of $10000 (class 1).  The file would be a seedling;		file instead of a sapling.  The bug was in the;		"create_eof" routine.  The code at label "not_tree";		looked like the following:;;		not_tree	txa;				cmp	#$0201;				bcc	make_seedling!;;		if should be the following:;;		not_tree	tya;				bne	make_sapling;				txa;				cmp	#$0201;				bcc	make_seedling!;; Jan  23 1990 by Rob Turner and Monte v03.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		Fixed a bug in set_eof.  If a file was being cut from a tree to a sapling then;		the first block of the file could be trashed by the routine "clr_to_boundary".;		The problem was that "TreeToSapling" filled the IO Buffer with the mark set to;		zero. This meant that the data in the I/O buffer was for the first block of the;		file.  Later when Clr_To_Boundary was called it assumed the block in the I/O;		buffer represented the LAST block of the file not the FIRST block of the file.;; Feb  04 1990 by Mensch v03.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		Fixed bug in dealloc_block.  Since a VCR keeps track of the;		lowest-numbered bitmap block that has any free blocks;		listed, dealloc_block must check whether it is freeing a;		block in a lower-numbered block than the one listed in the;		VCR, and update the VCR if necessary.  The bug was that the;		compare instruction used was a BEQ.  This has been changed;		to a BCS.;; Feb  26 1990 by Monte Benaresh v03.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		Fixed buf in process_path.  If the caller specified a device;		name in the path, the routine would first call check_vcr_first;		to see whether a VCR existed for that device, and if so, that;		VCR would be used for the rest of the call.  The problem is;		that if the user accesses a volume in a given device and then;		swaps in a different volume, when he specifies a device in;		the pathname process_path grabs the VCR for the first volume;		and we're stuck with it; the FST calls SCM through;		mount_message and insists that the user mount the first volume.;		It seems that eliminating the call to check_vcr_first is;		fine since that routine affects no globals, but allowed the;		process_path code to take a shortcut.  As long as id_disk;		gets called, everything should get set up fine.;; Mar  27 1990 by Monte Benaresh v3.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		The FLUSH call now supports two types of flush: a "full";		flush, which flushes a file's dirty data blocks and dirty;		directory blocks, and a "fast" flush which flushes dirty;		data blocks only. Note that FLUSH previously performed;		the "full" flush. The type of flush is specified in the;		parameter block by the caller. If this parameter is not;		given, the "full" flush is performed.;;		New FLUSH parameter block:;;			|---------------|;			|    P_COUNT	| (word);			|---------------|;			|    REF_NUM	| (word);			|---------------|;			|   FLUSH_TYPE	| (word);			|---------------|;;		Valid values for FLUSH_TYPE are:;			$0000 = full flush (default);			$8000 = fast flush;; Jun  21 1990 by Monte Benaresh v3.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		Fixed bug where SET_EOF would trash a file when a call;		was made to move the EOF back or the same on a file which;		has a sparse area at the end (i.e., sparsed-out blocks at;		the end of the block list) and the new EOF is still within;		the sparsed range.;;		The problem is that when SET_EOF sees that the new EOF;		is less than or equal to the old EOF, it calls;		setup_real_eof to calculate the effective EOF that is;		allocated for the file by scanning the index blocks;		for the last non-zero entry. The idea is that if blocks;		were pre-allocated for the file by specifying a;		non-zero EOF in the CREATE call, we will now be able to;		de-allocate them if the new EOF is smaller than the one;		specified then. However, if the end of the file is;		sparsed-out, setup_real_eof may come up with a value;		which is LESS than the new EOF. Since SET_EOF is now;		assuming that the task at hand is to deallocate blocks,;		major problems arise and bad stuff happens to the file.;;		The fix inserts a check at the end of setup_real_eof;		to see whether the "real" EOF value is less that the;		requested value. If this is the case then the new;		value is discarded and the old value is rounded-up to;		the next multiple of the blocksize, and this value is;		used by SET_EOF. This fix essentially simulates what;		would have happened if the end of the file was not;		sparse.;; July 9 1990 by Greg Branche;;		The original implementation of the "Fast Flush" call was;		bad.  The bitmap and directory were not being written to;		disk if either were dirty, which was causing corruption;		of the disk if the file was not properly flushed or;		closed.  (This could happen if the machine crashed while;		the file was open.)  This has been corrected by adding a;		"directory entry dirty" bit to the fcr_status word in the;		fcr.  Logic has also been added to insure the integrity of;		the bitmap and directory entry during a "Fast Flush" call.;;		SetFileInfo was not handling zeroed fields of the mod_date;		and create_date parameters correctly.  If the entire;		parameter is zeroed, it's supposed to either use the;		current date (if its the mod_date parm), or leave it;		alone (if its the create_date).  If only a portion of the;		field is zero, though, it should use the zero as the value;		to place in the directory entry.  This is now what happens.;;		Minor code compression performed to get the file back to;		it's original block size after adding the above fixes.;; Jul  20 1990 by Monte Benaresh v3.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		Fixed bug introduced by change to setup_real_eof on;		6/21/90. Whenever a file's storage type was "smaller";		than its EOF because the trailing portion was sparse,;		setting the EOF back enough to delete the second entry;		in an index block would cause the file to become a;		seedling, even if that index block was not the only;		one belonging to the file, and the volume would become;		corrupted (if the index block in question was "virtual";		due to its having been "sparsed out," the resulting;		seedling would own block 0 and deleting it would leave;		the boot block vulnerable to allocation).;;		The fix reverts the changes made to setup_real_eof;		because cut_block cannot work on a block whose logical;		position is out of the range of the current structure;		(e.g., beyond the 128K mark in a sapling). Instead,;		set_eof was modified to call setup_real_eof before;		deciding whether it needs to cut blocks, rather than;		deciding based on the logical EOF and then calling;		setup_real_eof only if the new EOF is less than the;		logical EOF. This ensures that set_eof will always;		compare the position of the last physically allocated;		block to the new EOF when deciding whether to cut;		blocks.;;		Another minor change in the same routine forces the;		new EOF to be updated into the FCR even when it is;		equal to the "old" EOF, because the "old" EOF is now;		the value calculated based on the physical allocation,;		and might not in fact be the same as the logical EOF;		recorded in the data structures.;; Jul  26 1990 by Matt Gulick & Monte Benaresh v3.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		Added terminator nulls to ProDOS partition type string,;		and added 2 to value of length byte. This correction;		ensures compatibility with the Macintosh Sys SW needed;		to use the ProDOS FSM.;; Aug   5 1990 by Monte Benaresh v3.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		Fixed a silly bug in dec_blk_count. When it decremented;		the block count it checked for the result being 0 or;		negative, and if so it would not store the new value or;		mark the directory as dirty, but just return with no;		error. The main problem is that the block count includes;		any index blocks and master index block, so a file which;		is not sparse can have a block count of up to $8081,;		which is negative and caused the block count to be;		"stuck" where is is during an EOF cutback.;;		The checks were simply removed since the block count is;		always valid if the FST is working properly, and because;		no meaningful error mechanism was ever implemented to;		handle an invalid block count.;; Aug   6-7 1990 by Monte Benaresh v3.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		Added a "jsr standard_req" to the load_index subroutine;		because it has always been lucky in the past that the;		drvr_req_cnt was correctly left over from previous;		calls. The resent bug fixes have caused the routine;		to be called right after a driver status call, in which;		case the "left over" value is $0006, which is wrong for;		a block read call. Also added a "jsr flush_file" in;		set_eof before the newly-added setup_real_eof call.;		This was done because those calls were originally made;		in that order before the resent fixes to set_eof and;		I don't want to take any chances.;;		Added code to setup_real_eof so that it now records;		the index block number in the FCR after calling;		load_index. Without this, the block number in the FCR;		did not match the block in the buffer, and a subsequent;		FLUSH would corrupt the disk.;; Aug   8 1990 by Monte Benaresh v3.07 for SD 5.0.3;;		Added a forgotten instruction to the 32-bit compare;		sequence added in the 7/21/90 fix to set_eof.  The;		"bne" instruction after the high word compare had;		carelessly been ommitted.;; Nov	6 1990 by Cheryl Ewy v3.08 for SD 6.0;;		Fixed a bug (BRC # 74604 and # 74530) in Create.  ;		If Create was attempting to add a resource fork to an;		existing file on a full disk, it would crash.  The problem;		was that after the code figured out how many free blocks;		there were on the disk, it didn't check to see if the;		result was 0 before continuing.;; Nov	11 1990 by Cheryl Ewy v3.08 for SD 6.0;;		Setup_params will now report a bad_path_syntax error if;		span1 or span2 is 0.;  ; Jan	22 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v3.08 for SD 6.0;; 		Enhancement - The Volume call will now setup the "volname" ;		output parameter if a "dup_volume" error is being returned.;;		Bug fix - Set_EOF was returning the wrong error code if;		the user tried to set the EOF < 0.;;		Bug fix - ChangePath was allowing illegal moves which;		resulted in a damaged disk.  The code was doing a;		case-sensitive compare on the pathnames instead of a;		case-insensitive compare.;; 		Enhancement - The ChangePath call will now allow a duplicate ;		volume to be renamed if a device name is used as the source ;		pathname.;;		Enhancement - Added support for the JudgeName call.;; Jan	25 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v3.08 for SD 6.0;;		Minor changes to JudgeName.;; Jan	31 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v3.08 for SD 6.0;;		Bug fix - JudgeName was not setting bit 13 of name_flags;		if the first character of the name was illegal.;; Feb	11 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v3.08 for SD 6.0;;		Modified JudgeName so that it won't remove any legal periods.;; Feb	13 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v3.08 for SD 6.0;;		Enhancement - If an option_list is supplied to SetFileInfo,;		and the FST ID of the list is HFS or AppleShare, and the;		file we are dealing with is a resource (extended) file,;		then the contents of the option list (the HFS Finder Info);		will be stored in the file's resource header after the ;		data fork's mini-entry.;		If an option_list is requested from GetFileInfo or Open or;		GetDirEntry, and the file we are dealing with is a resource;		file, and the resource header contains entries for the;		HFS Finder Info, then the HFS Finder Info will be returned;		in the option_list buffer and the FST ID of the list will;		be set to the ID of the HFS FST.;		This enhancement will allow HFS files to be copied to and;		from a ProDOS volume without losing their Macintosh creator;		and filetype information.;; Feb	15 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v3.08 for SD 6.0;;		Modified the previous enhancement.  The FST ID in the;		option_list will not be set to HFS so SetFileInfo;		will now also accept ProDOS option_lists.;; Feb	28 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v4.00 for SD 6.0;;		Changed version # to $8400 (version 4.00 prototype).;		Changed the max pcount allowed for Format and Erase_disk.;; Mar	 1 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v4.00 for SD 6.0;;  		Redefined the second field of the option_list to contain;		the actual data size when doing a SetFileInfo call.;; Mar	 5 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v4.00 for SD 6.0;;		Added support for error priorities.;; Mar	13 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v4.00 for SD 6.0;;		Bug fix (BRC # 74306) - GetFileInfo wasn't returning;		the latest 'eof' and 'blocks used' when dealing with;		an extended file which had an open fork.;; Mar	15 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v4.00 for SD 6.0;;		Bug fix (BRC # 73529) - ChangePath was allowing a;		'move and rename' on a locked file/dir.;;		Bug fix (BRC # 66663) - The backup bit for a directory;		was not set when the directory's contents changed.;; Mar	26 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v4.00 for SD 6.0;;		Bug fix (BRC # 77894) - Attempting to rename the startup;		volume to an illegal name (bad syntax) resulted in the ;		boot prefix being changed to the illegal name before the ;		syntax error was discovered and reported.;;		Bug fix (BRC # 53517) - Renaming a volume which had the;		same name as the boot volume caused the boot prefix to;		be changed to the new name.;; Apr	 5 1991 by Cheryl Ewy v4.00 for SD 6.0;;		Bug fix - When the backup bit for a volume directory was set;		by slug_ripple or cleared by clear_backup, the vcr wasn't ;		updated which caused the FST to think there was a duplicate ;		volume the next time it compared the volume header to the vcr.;; Apr   14 1991 by Dave Lyons v4.00 for SD 6.0;;		Added code to JudgeName to call StringToText on the input;		text before the rest of the processing.  This way if there;		are foreign characters or special symbols in the input, they;		get converted into their closest text representations before;		we change invalid characters to periods, etc.  For example,;		"RŽsumŽ Plusª" (which was previously translated to "R.sum.Plus");		is now translated to "Resume.Plus.TM".;; Apr	29 1991 by Dave Lyons v4.00 for SD 6.0;;		In JudgeName after my StringToText call, added check to see;		if the string has become null.  This case was going off into;		the weeds.;; Jun 21, 1991	by Greg Branche;;		In convert_time routine, I added logic to limit the allowable;		  range of year values from 00-99, in accordance with ProDOS;		  Tech Note #28.;		Altered unpack_time so that year values in the range of 0-39;		  are adjusted to 100-139, so that they end up returned as;		  2000-2039 (again, for TN #28).;; Aug 7, 1991	by Dave Lyons;;		Added conditional code based on AllowLCPeriods.  If you set;		  this symbol to 1, then the FST allows space characters;		  in names, which are really "lowercase periods" on disk.;; Aug 12, 1991	by Jack Valois;;		Changed the setup_params and change_path routines to ensure;		  that the error prioriity gets setup before span2 is checked.;; Oct 3, 1991  by Jack Valois;;		Moved the check for moving a parent into a child in the ChangePath;		call, from the validate_paths routine into the main ChangePath ;		routine.  The problem was that ProDOS was making the child check;		in a different manner that what is done in HFS and AppleShare.;		This resulted in a different error being returned for the very;		same case, depending on which filesystem it was on.  The check;		for a child should be made after the check to see if the dest.;		allready exsists.;; Oct 10, 1991 by Jack Valois;;		Moved check for if spans are valid for all calls that use a ;		pathname until after we have identified the target volume as ;		a ProDOS volume.  This will insure that the error_priority;		get sets properly if the target is a ProDOS volume.;; Dec 5, 1991 by Mark Day;;		Added a third parameter to class 1 change_path: flags.;		If bit 15 is set and the source path is a device number;		only, then assume it is a duplicate volume and build a fake;		VCR, just as if there was already a duplicate VCR with open;		files.  This allows disk-copy programs (including Finder);		to rename a disk without making the OS think the source;		disk was renamed.;; Dec 20, 1991 by Mark Day;;		There is a disk corruption bug caused by a device driver;		falsely returning a disk-switched status from a device;		status call after the pathname has already been found;		(you'd have to be REALLY quick to switch the disk).  This;		results in the VCR being disposed if there are no open;		files.  The FST then accesses what used to be the VCR,;		and things go down hill from there.  The status call is;		made as a result of calling chk_disk_protect. Now;		chk_disk_protect calls mount_volume after finding the;		disk switched condition.  The side-effect is that we;		won't detect the write-protect setting immediately and;		might have to wait until the first attempt to write to;		the device to get the write protected error.;; Jan 7, 1992 by Greg Branche;		Added logic in vol_to_buffer (part of Build_Path) which;		checks the length of the volume name being parsed out;		to ensure that it contains no more than 15 characters.;		If it exceeds 15 characters, we exit immediately with;		a bad_path_syntax error.;; Jan 22, 1992 by Mark Day;		In the PROC data, moved all the non-zero data items to;		the start of the PROC so that all of the zero bytes would;		be contiguous.  This results in a one block savings in;		disk space due to a block being sparsified out (i.e. it;		results in a block of all zeroes that doesn't actually;		get written on ProDOS disks).;; Jan 27, 1992 by Mark Day;		When convert_time converts a class 1 date/time to a;		ProDOS date/time, if the year is outside the range;		1940..2039 (i.e. year outside 40..139), the date/time;		will be set to all zeroes (i.e. unknown).  Note: I had;		to make an ugly hack here: I return the time as $8000;		(an invalid bit is set) so it would be non-zero and other;		parts of the FST wouldn't assume no date/time was supplied;;		This invalid bit will get stripped before the date is;		written to disk.;;		The 10/3/91 change caused temp_ptr and temp2_ptr to be;		reset after valid_change carefully set them up (like;		pointing past a device and/or volume name).  This meant;		that a ChangePath from ".device:A" to ":Vol:A:B", where;		volume A was on .device, would succeed.  Moved the;		code that checks for the move-into-own-child back into;		valid_change where it now sets a flag cp_to_child which;		will be checked later.;; Feb 5, 1992 by Mark Day;		When doing a Change_Path with bit 15 of the flags set;		(the "trust me" bit), it will do a swap_out on the device;		just before building its fake VCR so there won't be two;		online VCR's for the same device.;; Feb 7, 1992 by Mark Day;		Changed version number in header and version string;		to version 04.00 (final).;; Feb 28, 1992 by Dave Lyons and Greg Branche;		Modified Mark's December 20, 1991 mod so that when;		chk_disk_protect calls mount_volume, it preserves and;		restores the first block of GBUF.  This fixes volume-;		corruption problems when disk-switched occurs (on HD20s,;		for example).;; Feb 28, 1992 by Greg Branche;		When the pathname span check was removed from setup_parms,;		format and erase calls were broken.  If a volume name was;		supplied that had a length of a multiple of 16 characters,;		the calls would trash 64K of memory as it copied the volume;		name into the directory entry template.  This is being fixed;		by adding calls to check_span immediately after the calls;		to setup_parms.;;======================================================================;; BEGIN 6.0.1;; July 13, 1992 by Dave Lyons (v4.02d6);		Fixed a bug in slow_read that made multiple-character;		newline mode unreliable.  Each time it moved to a new;		block of data it was forgetting about one more character;		off the end of the newline list!  Bug reported by;		Bill Heinaman.;;======================================================================